  "findings": {
    "F1_degeneracy_exact": "For N=2 with spacing D, s(P)=0 for every P=D/m (m>=1, D/m>=p_min): an exact global tie. P_hat is unidentifiable; R is identified and saturates at 1.",
    "F2_realized_convention_is_not_strictly_longest_P": "argmin resolves the tie by floating-point rounding of 1-R at the ~1e-16 level, not by a period preference. Even IN RANGE (D <= p_max, where P=D is a grid point and m=1 is reachable), m=1 is selected in only 74.2%-75.2% of draws; the rest land on sub-harmonics purely through float tie-breaking. The roadmap's 'argmin -> longest-P is the realized convention' is therefore approximately, not exactly, true, and is corrected here. The two strata are reported separately because their aggregate depends on the data-dependent spacing distribution.",
    "F3_in_range_degeneracy_is_absorbed_by_the_sealed_tolerance": "recovery._period_match accepts m in {2,3} (flagged harmonic), which covers 98.1%-98.6% of in-range N=2 seeds; only 1.4%-1.9% leak to m>=4 and miss the sealed period predicate. Every such leak is a RECALL cost, never a false positive.",
    "F3b_out_of_range_is_a_different_failure_entirely": "When D > p_max the true spacing lies outside the sealed search range, so no harmonic of D need fall on the grid: 99.3%-99.5% of these draws return a period that is not a harmonic of D at all, and R does NOT saturate (R_saturated_at_1 = [False, False]). This is a grid-RANGE limitation, not the tie-breaking degeneracy, and it presents to the FAP as an ordinary weak fold rather than as a perfect one.",
    "F4_fap_validity": "The FAP compares R, which is invariant on the tie set, and the surrogates are scored by the identical degenerate procedure. The degeneracy therefore cannot inflate the false-alarm rate; it costs period accuracy only.",
    "F5_what_the_N2_fap_actually_tests": "With obs_R saturated at 1, the N=2 FAP reduces to the null probability of a surrogate producing only 2-3 events -- a rarity test, not a coherence test.",
    "F6_reproducibility_caveat": "Because the tie is broken by IEEE-754 rounding, the selected harmonic is deterministic for a given input and numpy build but is NOT guaranteed stable across architectures or numpy versions. Any future re-run should pin the convention explicitly (e.g. argmin over scores rounded to 1e-12, then longest P) rather than inherit float noise. Sealed Phase-I results are unaffected: they were produced once, on one build, and are preserved verbatim."
  },
